    "result_text": "All 63 tests pass and the build succeeds.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the level-ceiling rule: **a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.**\n\nThe failing test (`Cp51Tests`) creates a second owner in the same household (same last name + postcode) *without* `sharesHousehold` and expects it to be created and level-capped. This was previously rejected with `409`. Reconciling it against the existing tests pinned down the exact behavior:\n\n- `Cp10`/`Cp35`: a same-household owner with **no distinguishing email** stays a `409` household duplicate.\n- `Cp51`: the second owner supplies a **distinct email** (one no household member holds), so they are an identifiable new person \u2014 admitted, but level-capped.\n- `Cp23`/`Cp39`: explicitly-declared (`sharesHousehold`) members keep their full level (e.g. 3) and must **not** be capped.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `membershipLevelCap` field; `getMembershipLevel()` now lowers the derived level to the cap when one is set (never below 1, since a cap is always max-member-level + 1 \u2265 2).\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 the household-duplicate block now admits a non-declared member that `introducesDistinctEmail(...)`, and for such non-`sharesHousehold` joins it sets `membershipLevelCap = max(householdMembers' level) + 1`. Declared (`sharesHousehold`) joins are untouched, preserving `Cp23`/`Cp39`.\n\n3. **Schema files** (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) \u2014 added the `membership_level_cap` column so the cap persists and is reflected on subsequent `GET`s.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 documented the cap on the `membershipLevel` field.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 63, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: the requirement text only describes the cap, but making `Cp51` pass without breaking `Cp10`/`Cp35`/`Cp23`/`Cp39` required inferring two things the tests encode but the prose doesn't spell out \u2014 (a) a distinct-email owner is admitted into an existing household rather than rejected, and (b) the cap applies to those undeclared joins, not to `sharesHousehold` joins. If either of those inferred behaviors doesn't match your intent, let me know and I can adjust.",
